"A class diagram describes types, their members, and structural relationships using explicitly chosen UML notation.\n\nUse when: Review domain vocabulary and relationship constraints. Explain a small set of implementation types and their contracts.\n\nAvoid when: Concrete instance values or message order are required.\n\nVisual grammar: Compartmented boxes name classes, attributes, and operations. Associations carry role names and multiplicities at their ends. Distinct arrowheads encode generalization, realization, or dependency.\n\nBuild: Choose a conceptual or implementation view and state its scope. Select only types needed for the reader’s question. Add association roles and multiplicities before optional member details. Place related types together and route connectors through empty gutters.\n\nCheck: Multiplicity is read correctly from each association end. Composition denotes ownership semantics rather than visual grouping. Generalization points toward the more general classifier. Member text, arrowheads, and association labels have separate space.\n\nPitfalls: Dumping every getter and setter into an unreadable class inventory. Using association, aggregation, and composition interchangeably.\n\nExample: Order contains OrderLines and references Customer; Order.submit() states behavior on its owning entity.\n\nAlternatives: Object diagram, Domain aggregate diagram."
